---
description: >
  TEMPORAL AGENT RULES - /agents repo

  These rules are MANDATORY for the Navi temporal worker codebase in /agents.

  General Architecture:
  - All agent workflows and activities MUST be deterministic and idempotent
  - No workflow may directly use wall-clock time, randomness, UUIDs, or non-deterministic APIs; use Temporal workflow APIs for all such logic (e.g. workflow.now())
  - All operations with external side effects (outbound API calls, updates, notifications) must take idempotency keys from workflow context
  
  Activities & Tools:
  - Write all tools as separate files in agents/tools/. Each tool is a Temporal activity
  - Activities MUST: Be idempotent (safe on retry; use idempotency keys/upserts for side effects), Support retries with exponential backoff as defined in config/fly.toml (never hardcoded), Heartbeat every 5–10 seconds if potentially long-running (over 30s), Never block for sleep/waiting purposes—use timers only in workflows
  - NEVER use activity-based waits. Use workflow.sleep() exclusively when you want time delays or schedule future actions
  - MAKE sure when adding a new tool to connect all the pipes necessary from the workflow to the tool, make sure to read through the workflow code from start to finish and make sure we are piping in the required activities and helper functions, pay EXTRA attention to this
  
  Workflow Patterns:
  - Workflows MUST be deterministic: always produce same results for same inputs, even if replayed
  - Use continue_as_new for long-running workflows (reset history every 100-1000 events, or when state exceeds 100KB)
  - All workflow state passed to continue-as-new must be serializable and as compact as possible (summarize/compress long arrays, strip large data)
  - NO workflow timeouts—agent workflows must be indefinite by default
  - Errors in activities DO NOT kill workflows—catch, record to SYSTEM message, then continue
  
  Agent Communication:
  - Agents poll the backend database for new messages. Do NOT use Temporal signals to deliver user input, feedback, or chat messages
  - Message flow: User → Backend saves to DB → Worker polls DB
  - Only use direct DB polling for agent context updates
  
  Testing Requirements:
  - When adding/modifying a tool, agent workflow, or activity: You MUST test retry/idempotency by intentionally causing activity retries and verifying no duplicate side effects, Simulate worker failures and check workflows resume and replay deterministically, Test continue-as-new boundaries for state handoff
  - Always use /agents/scripts/quick-test.sh for lifecycle and workflow validation before PR/merge
  
  Configuration & Limits:
  - Concurrency, retry, and batch size settings MUST be controlled via environment or config (fly.toml)—never directly in source files
  - Do NOT hardcode task queue names, connection strings, or API keys in code
  
  Code Organization:
  - Main workflows live in agents/workflows/
  - Agent-owned logic only: never couple backend-specific code to agent worker
  - Tools/activities = one file per tool/function, minimal coupling, explicit state passing
  
  Commit & Docs Policy:
  - Do NOT create markdown or plaintext docs anywhere in the repo; all documentation must reside as code comments or in this YAML frontmatter rule pack
  - All significant workflow, tool, or architecture design decisions must be evident from code structure and comments—avoid non-code documentation
  
  Prohibited Patterns:
  - No activity-based waits/sleeps
  - No hardcoded queue names, timeouts, or keys
  - No Temporal signal-based user messaging
  - No workflow timeouts
  - No unbounded state in continue-as-new
  - No direct random/uuid usage in workflows
  
  Enforcement:
  - Any PR that introduces or modifies agent workflows, activities, or state-handling logic MUST show compliance with these rules. For any exception, explicit written justification in PR is required

apply:
  when: always
---
